1,0$

1,2 $ Veil present, leaving a membranaceous annulus on stipe or flocks adhering to the margin of pileus, at least in young basidiocarps
1,3 $ Veil absent or presence of veil unknown

2,cs1 $ Veil leaving a membranaceous annulus on stipe $$s"Sect. Pholiotina"
2,cs1,n $Sect. Pholiotina : Veil leaving a membranaceous annulus on stipe.The annulus is loosely attached to the stipe and radially striate-sulcate on upper side. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform or utriform (sect.Pholiotina) ///N.B.Theannulus may be ephemeral. Occasionally theannulus is broken in several parts, which may be adhering to thepileus margin. Suchremains of the veilare usually striateat one side, thicker and less numerous than on the species keyedoutinkeytwo (sect.Vestitaeandlntermediae). In othercases the annulus may have disappeared completely in mature basidiocarpsoritis adheringtasurrounding vegetation. Key four isavailable for identification of older basidiocarps.

2,cs2 $ Veil leaving flocks adhering to the margin of pileus$$s"sect.Vestitae and Intermediae "
2,cs2,n $sect.Vestitae and Intermediae : Veil leaving flocks adhering to pileus margin, occasionally also on stipe. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform, sublecythiform or utriform. (sect.Vestitae and Intermediae) ///N.B.The flocks of the veil in this group are thin, fibrillose, andneversulcate-striate.The yareloosely attached to the pileus surface and may be easily washedoffbyrain. Therefore, they are often lacking in older basidiocarps. Such basidiocarps may be identified with key four. See also remarks under key one.



3,cs3 $ Veil definitely absent (young basidiocarps available); stipeus ually entirely pruinose, with numerous caulocystidia; pileocystidia often present $$s"Sect. Piliferae"$$s"sect.Piliferae "
3,cs3,n $ sect.Piliferae : Veil absent. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform or utriform (sect.Piliferae).///N.B.The veilin species of Pholiotina is ephernerous and may be absent in older basidiocarps or underwet conditions. See notes under key one and two. One may try to identfy such specimens with the present key. When the presence of veil is doubt ful it is better to use keyfour, which treats all species of this genus.



3,0 $ Presence of veil uncertain ==> KEY FOUR (all species of Pholiotina) $all species of Pholiotina$f"Pholiotina_FAN_Key 4.cle"

Key ONE 
Veil leaving a membranaceous annulus on stipe.The annulus is loosely attached to the stipe and radially striate-sulcate on upper side. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform or utriform (sect.Pholiotina) 
N.B.Theannulus may be ephemeral. Occasionally theannulus is broken in several parts, which may be adhering to thepileus margin. Suchremains of the veilare usually striateat one side, thicker and less numerous than on the species keyedoutinkeytwo (sect.Vestitaeandlntermediae). In othercases the annulus may have disappeared completely in mature basidiocarpsoritis adheringtasurrounding vegetation. Key four isavailable for identification of older basidiocarps.

Cs1,0 $ Key One (sect.Pholiotina)

1,2 $ Spores 9.0-15.0 x 5.0-7.5 µm,on the average longer than 10.0 µm
1,4 $ Spores6.5-10.0 x 4.0-5.5 µm, on the average less than10.0 µm long

2,0 $ Basidia2-spored $ Ph. teneroides
2,3 $ Basidia4-spored

3,0 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform with long, cylindrical or tapering neck, 3.0-4.0 µm broad 5. $ Ph. vexans
3,0 $ Cheilocystidia utriform with rounded apex, 7.0-12.0 µm broad 6. $ Ph. utricystidiata

4,0 $ Spores without germ pore; basidiocarps usually vernal 3. $ Ph. aporos
4,5 $ Spores with germ pore; basidiocarps usually in summer and autumn

5,0 $ Cheilocystidia lanceolate to lageniform, gradually passing into a narrow, cylindrical or tapering neck, -3.5(4.0) µm wide, not capitate;spores with distinct germ pore, 1.0-1.5 µm wide.4. $ Ph. rugosa
5,6 $ Cheilocystidia either predominantly subcylindrical to narrowly lageniform with flexuose, obtuse to subcapitate neck,2.0-5.0 µm wide, or predominantly utriform to clavate; spores with small germ pore up to 1.0 µm wide

6,0 $ Cheilocystidia subcylindrical to narrowly lageniform with obtuse, often flexuose neck, apex 2.0-5.0 µm wide 1.	$ Ph. arrhenii
6,0 $ Cheilocystidia intermixed lageniform, utriform and clavate, the latter with apex 5.0-15.0 µm wide 2.	$ Ph. hadrocystis


Key Two 
Veil leaving flocks adhering to pileus margin, occasionally also on stipe. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform, sublecythiform or utriform. (sect.Vestitae and Intermediae) 
N.B.The flocks of the veil in this group are thin, fibrillose, andneversulcate-striate.The yareloosely attached to the pileus surface and may be easily washedoffbyrain. Therefore, they are often lacking in older basidiocarps. Such basidiocarps may be identified with key four. See also remarks under key one.

Cs2,0 $ Key Two (sect.Vestitae and Intermediae)

1,0 $Cheilocystidia lageniform to sublecythiform, tapering in to a narrow neck with well-delimited capitulum, 4.0-6.µm wide; spores usually in part phaseoliform 14.$ Ph. brunnea
1,2 $ Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, utriform or lageniform, occasionally subcapitate but without well-delimited capitulum; spores not phaseoliform

2,3 $ Spores without germ pore, sometimes with slightly paler apical spot (callus)
2,4 $ Spores with distinct germ pore

3,0 $ Flocks of veil fibrillose and thin, usually large, fringing the entire margin of pileus; basidiocarps usually in summer and autumn 8.$ Ph. vestita
3,0 $ Flocks of veil submembranaceous, thick, often striate-sulcate, in one or more irregular patches adhering to pileus margin; basidiocarps usually in spring 3.$ Ph. aporos

4,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly utriform to clavate. Spores minutely verruculose under oil-immersion lens, in some collections seemingly smooth (but verruculose under SEM)13.$ Ph. utriformis
4,5 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly subcylindrical to lageniform, often subcapitate. Spores smooth. Insufficiently unravelled group of Ph. velata; see also notes under that species $$s"Groupe velata"

5,6 $ Spores on the average 7.0-8.3 µm long
5,7 $ Spores on the average(8.5)9.0-10.6 µm long

6,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform with slender, often subcapitate neck. Pileus centre usually rather pale when moist: yellow-brown, ochre-brown, orangey brown 9.$ Ph. velata
6,0 $ Cheilocystidia more variable, lageniform, utriform, clavate and subcylindrical. Pileus centre usually rather dark when moist: reddish brown to rusty brown 10.$ Ph. exannulata

7,0 $ Cheilo cystidia lagenifonn with long cylindrical neck,3.0-5.0(6.0)µm wide, often in part subcapitate 11.$ Ph. nemoralis
7,0,N$/// = P. nemoralis var. dendromarginata sec Hausknecht (2009), Conocybe-Pholitina:624
7,0 $ Cheilocystidia more variable, lageniform, utriform, clavate and subcylindrical 12. $ Ph. mutabilis
7,0,N$/// = P. nemoralis sec Hausknecht (2009), Conocybe-Pholitina:621

KEY THREE 
Veil absent. Cheilocystidia subcylindrical, lageniform or utriform (sect.Piliferae).
N.B.The veilin species of Pholiotina is ephernerous and may be absent in older basidiocarps or underwet conditions. See notes under key one and two. One may try to identfy such specimens with the present key. When the presence of veil is doubt ful it is better to use keyfour, which treats all species of this genus.

Cs3,0 $ KEY THREE (sect.Piliferae).

1,2 $ Basidiocarp with green or blue tinge on pileus and/or stipe
1,3 $ Basidiocarps without green or blue tinge

2,0 $ Pileus greyish brown to reddish brown without blue colours; stipe whitish, at base becoming bluish grey to blue-green when bruised and in age; spores7.0-9.0 x 4.0-5.5 µm 22.	 $ Ph. cyanopus
2,0 $ Pileus blue-green, at least at centre; stipe whitish; spores8.0-11.5 x 4.5-6.5 µm 23. $ Ph. aeruginosa

3,0 $ Pileus strongly plicate-sulcate up to halfway the radius 21. $ Ph. sulcata
3,4 $ Pileus not sulcate, often translucently striate or rugulose near centre

4,0 $ Spores 11.0-14.0 x 6.5-8.0 µm; habitat on dung 20. $ Ph. coprophila
4,5 $ Spores less than 10.5 µm long; habitat on soil

5,6 $ Basidiocarps small: pileus 4-20 mm wide, stipe10-45 x 0.5-2 mm; pileus strongly translucently stiate at least up to half of the radius when moist and fresh
5,10 $ Basidiocarps relatively stout: pileus 15-55 mm wide, stipe 25-75 x 2-7 mm; pileus not translucently striate or weakly striate at margin only

6,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly utriform and clavate; spores minutely verruculose under oil-immersion lens, in some collections seemingly smooth (but verruculose under SEM)13. $ Ph. utriformis
6,7 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly lageniform or lanceolate to subcylindrial; spores smooth

7,0 $ Stipe white fibrillose-striate, not pruinose; spores 7.0-9.0 x 4.5-5.5 µm, on the average7.8-8.5 µm long, with slightly eccentric germ pore; pileocystidia absent or scarce and obscure 22.	 $ Ph. cyanopus
7,8 $ Stipe pruinose to minutely pubescent, not fibrillose-striate; spores with central germ pore; pileocystidia numerous and striking

8,0 $ Spores7.5-11.0 x 4.5-6.0 µm, on the average longer than 8.2 µm; clamp-connections present; pileocystidia 37-95 x 8.0-16 µm  19. $ Ph. aberrans
